<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Huron</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Huron is the second largest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> by area, lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and Michigan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It was named by the French after the Huron, or Wendat, who lived near Georgian Bay, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> reached its shores in 1615. It is joined to Lake Michigan by the Straits of Mackinac and drains south into Lake Erie.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-huron.jpg" alt="Lake Huron" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Huron_in_winter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
